# Line-ending policy.
#
# Every tracked text file is committed as LF (the whole history is LF), so pin
# LF for checkouts too.
#
# What this does NOT do: stop an editor or file-sync tool from rewriting the
# worktree to CRLF. `git status` still reports those files as modified —
# gitattributes cannot make a CRLF worktree look clean. Verified 2026-10-16 by
# simulating the rewrite with and without this file: the `M` status is
# identical in both cases.
#
# What it DOES do is stop that rewrite from reaching the repository. With this
# file `git add` normalizes to LF, so the CRLF can never be committed; without
# it the same `git add` stores CRLF verbatim, which is how a 100+ file "changed"
# commit gets made out of pure line-ending noise. `git diff` also starts
# emitting the explicit hint "CRLF will be replaced by LF" instead of differing
# silently.
#
# So the symptom is still possible (it hit all 123 tracked files on
# 2026-10-16) — it is just no longer harmful. Diagnose an EOL-only dirty tree
# with `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol --stat`: empty means nothing but the
# carriage return differs, and `git restore .` is the fix.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Upstream vendored spec files that are genuinely CRLF in the repository
# (awesome-design-md, MIT — see docs/README.md). `-text` opts them out of
# normalization so they keep their original bytes instead of showing up as
# permanently modified.
docs/design/DESIGN.md -text
docs/design/LICENSE.awesome-design-md -text

# Binary assets: never line-ending converted.
*.png binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
